<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claude Lorrain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1600–<a href="/years/1682">1682</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Claude Gellée</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claude Lorrain, born Claude Gellée at Chamagne in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, was a landscape painter who spent nearly all his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His idealised views of the Roman countryside and of harbours bathed in golden light became the model of the classical landscape. They strongly influenced painters and garden designers, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, into the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claude-lorrain.jpg" alt="Claude Lorrain" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claude Lorrain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Gell%C3%A9e,_dit_le_Lorrain,_Self-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
